Dolby (AIDL)
============

Dolby Atmos / DAX3 for Evolution-X, branch `cnb-aidl`. Used by
`device_xiaomi_sm8450-common` (`cnb-6.6`, POCO F5 / marble).

Stack
-----
- DMS: `vendor.dolby.dms` AIDL (`IDms/default`), `vendor.dolby.dms.service`
  + `libdmshal`, `libdlbdsservice`, `libdapparamstorage`, `libdlbpreg`
- Effects (audio effect HAL V2): `libswdapaidl` (software DAP),
  `libdlbvolaidl` (volume listener), `libswgamedapaidl` (game DAP)
- Codec2: `vendor.dolby.media.c2-default-service-dax` (AC3/EAC3/EAC3-JOC/AC4)
- App: XiaomiDolby (Lunaris-based), replaces MusicFX/AudioFX
- Dolby Vision (optional, `TARGET_INCLUDES_DolbyVision`): HEVC/AVC decoders and
  HEVC encoder (`c2.dolby.*`), served over AIDL, see "Dolby Vision" below

Blobs
-----
DAX3_3.7.0.8_r1. The whole stack is one Sony drop, the Xperia 1 VII (pdx256):
DMS (`vendor.dolby.dms.service`, `libdmshal`, `libdlbdsservice`,
`libdapparamstorage`, `libdlbpreg`, `vendor.dolby.dms-V1-ndk`,
`vendor.dolby.hardware.dms@2.0`), Codec2 (`vendor.dolby.media.c2-default-service-dax`,
`libcodec2_store_dolby`, `libdeccfg`, the AC-4 and DD+ decoders), the effect
libraries and the tuning files. Every host library is the build the effect
libraries were linked against, so no part of the stack is a different Dolby
build than its neighbours.
https://github.com/PinkSerenity/proprietary_vendor_sony_pdx256 (lineage-22.1)

The only blob not from that drop is
`libaudio_aidl_conversion_common_ndk_prebuilt.so`, from kunzite
(https://github.com/kunzite-stuff/vendor_xiaomi_kunzite, cnb). It is the same
conversion library under a different name, because AOSP already builds a module
called `libaudio_aidl_conversion_common_ndk`, so the Sony file cannot be
installed under its own name. It exports the same 178 symbols as the Sony one,
including the three that `libswdapaidl` and `libswgamedapaidl` import.

DAP: only the software DAP is shipped (`libswdapaidl`, `libswgamedapaidl`).
It processes on the CPU and needs no DSP or GEF support.

- No hardware DAP (`libhwdapaidl`) is shipped. It drives the DSP through GEF.
  The pdx256 build expects the new GEF interface, and the vendor
  `libqtigefar` (Sony-era) does not provide it, so parameters never apply and
  the device callback crashes the audio HAL. The kunzite build runs, but it
  re-pushes the full parameter set to every output device class on each
  parameter update (about 400 batches in 50 seconds after boot in one test
  log, which floods the audio HAL log), and PAL stores the baseline under a
  graph that has no DAP module, so the deep-buffer stream never receives it.
  The sm8850 build uses module tag `0x0C000033`, which the marble ACDB does not
  carry, and installs under the same file name.
- Both effect libraries link `libaudio_aidl_conversion_common_ndk_prebuilt`
  (shipped here).
- The tuning is read from `/vendor/etc/dolby/dax-default.xml`.
- Cross-checked against `paulblazer15/vendor_sony_dolby` (17, XQ-FS72): every
  Dolby blob there has the same BuildId as the pdx256 one. It only differs in
  carrying the `propIsMatched` patch below, and in shipping `liboem_specific.so`
  (a single `dd_data` symbol that nothing in this stack links), which is not
  taken.

Binary fixes applied to the pdx256 `libswdapaidl.so` / `libswgamedapaidl.so`:
- `DlbGenClass2::propIsMatched()` forced true (`mov w0, w19` -> `mov w0, #1`).
  Same patch as vendor_sony_dolby 5dd32bc, same offsets
  (`0x2a81fc` / `0x2982a4`).

Properties (`dolby.mk`):
- `ro.vendor.dolby.dax.version=DAX3_3.7.0.8_r1`
- `ro.vendor.dolby.dap_bypassed_on_tiny_volume=0`: the default (1) latches DAP
  into bypass at boot while the volume is low and the profiles do nothing
  (found on emerald, crdroid-17 b868d52).

Init: `dms-service.rc` declares `interface aidl vendor.dolby.dms.IDms/default`.

App fixes (from halcyon `packages_halcyon_apps_Dolby`):
- Never `setEnabled(false)` on the DAP: AudioFlinger STOPs the HAL effect, the
  drain returns -ENODATA, EffectHalAidl maps it to DEAD_OBJECT and the
  instance cannot be re-enabled. Use bypass param 18 + enable param instead.
- Profile parameters are write-only registers on this DAP (read back 0), so
  the getters read the saved prefs first.

Tuning (marble stock)
---------------------
`configs/dax-default.xml` is the stock marble image's file
(missi_phone_cn V816.0.14.0.UMRCNXM), so profiles, IEQ and speaker/headphone
tuning are marble's. It is the older 3.3.0 format; the pdx256 files are 3.4.0.
The pdx256 `libdlbdsservice` parser is version-aware and reads 3.3.0 through
its own path, so it is used as is:

| | pdx256 (3.4.0) | marble stock (3.3.0) |
|---|---|---|
| format / tool version | 3.4.0 / 3.6.0 | 3.3.0 / 3.5.0 |
| IEQ preset ids | numeric, `id="2" name="balanced"` | string, `id="ieq_balanced"` |
| IEQ reference in profile | `<include ieq_preset="2"/>` | `<include preset="ieq_balanced"/>` |
| profiles | 0-7 | 0-8 (8 = Voice) |
| `headphone-virtualizer-mode`, `speaker-virtualizer-mode`, `advanced-{headphone,speaker}-virtualizer-rendering-config`, `advanced-speaker-virtualizer-start-bin` | present | absent (3.4-only) |
| `speaker_landscape_180` tuning | present | absent |

What the parser does (disassembly of `libdlbdsservice.so`):
- `parsePreset` and `parseProfileEndpoint` compare the file version with
  `3.3.0`: an exact 3.3.0 file takes the `preset` include attribute (string
  ids), any other version takes `ieq_preset` (numeric ids).
- `parseProfile` gates the virtualizer mode / rendering config tags on `3.4.`,
  so a 3.3.0 file without them is expected.
- `getIeqPresetIdByName` maps preset names by substring (`balanced` -> 2
  seen), which is how string ids such as `ieq_balanced` resolve to the 1/2/3
  the app sends.

So the file does not need converting. Keep the header at exactly `3.3.0`: any
other version selects the `ieq_preset` path and breaks the includes. Static
analysis only, not confirmed on device:

```
logcat -s dolby DolbyManagerService TuningFileParser | grep -iE "XML|IEQ|Invalid"
```
`XML parses error` or `Invalid IEQ Preset Id` would mean it was rejected.

Note: marble's volume leveler is enabled in 26 profile/endpoint entries.
vendor_sony_dolby disables it in all of them (`volume-leveler-enable` ->
false); not done here, to keep stock tuning.

Dolby Vision
------------
Optional, off by default. Set `TARGET_INCLUDES_DolbyVision := true` before
`hardware/dolby/dolby.mk` is inherited. Everything below is behind that flag.

Blobs: the marble stock image (missi_phone_cn V816.0.14.0.UMRCNXM, Android 14),
from https://github.com/gm-stuffs/redmi_marble_dump. They are HIDL Codec2 1.0
blobs: `c2.dolby.{avc.dec,avc.sec.dec,client,egl,hevc.dec,hevc.enc,hevc.sec.dec,store}`,
`libdolbyvision` and `libdolbyottcameracontrol` (64-bit only). They link the
plain `libstagefright_foundation`, and none of them lists `libcodec2_shim` or
`libcodec2_hidl_shim` as NEEDED. `dolby_vision.cfg` is also marble's (Tmax 900)
and `media_codecs_dolby_vision.xml` is the decoder/encoder list.

The stock services are not used. `media.c2.hal.selection=aidl` on 6.6 makes
`Codec2Client` list AIDL stores only, and the QTI store is AIDL only, so two
services bridge the HIDL blobs:

- `vendor.dolbyvision.media.c2-service` (`codec2/DolbyC2Service.cpp`) serves
  `c2.dolby.store` as AIDL `android.hardware.media.c2.IComponentStore/default10`.
  `default9` is the Dolby audio store and `default2` the QTI audio store.
  `c2.dolby.client` converts each `C2Work` with the HIDL `objcpy()`, which only
  accepts HIDL-pool-backed blocks, so the service copies every linear input
  buffer into a `VER_HIDL` pool block before the Dolby component queues it.
  The original AIDL-backed buffers are kept by frame index and put back into
  each work item (done or flushed) before the AIDL listener converts it, as
  the copies have no AIDL `BufferPoolData`.
- `vendor.dolbyvision.media.c2-qti-hidl-service` (`codec2/QtiC2HidlStore.cpp`)
  loads `libqcodec2_core.so` and serves the QTI store over HIDL
  `IComponentStore/default`, which is where `c2.dolby.client` looks for the
  hardware decoder. The framework never lists it. It calls the store factory
  through vtable slot 2, so it depends on the layout of the vendor
  `libqcodec2_core.so`.

`c2.dolby.client` imports `objcpy()` under its old, pre-template mangled name.
`libcodec2_shim` (`hardware/lineage/compat`) exports it and forwards to the
platform function. The blobs do not list it as NEEDED, so it is in the
client's `shared_libs` for `check_elf` and the c2-service links it explicitly. Do not add a shim that redefines it with another signature: it would
win symbol resolution and truncate the sender pointer.

Device wiring, in addition to the audio steps below:
- `TARGET_INCLUDES_DolbyVision := true`
- `media.c2.hal.selection=aidl`
- include the Vision codecs next to the audio ones:
  `<Include href="media_codecs_dolby_vision.xml" />`
- `ro.vendor.display.dolbyvision.support=true`, read by the `hardware_qcom_display`
  composer, so HWC advertises `Hdr::DOLBY_VISION` (the Dolby Vision tone mapping
  runs in the Codec2 EGL pipeline, not in `libsdmextension`)
- `mediacodec` may reach the framework sensor service (LightSense, ambient
  adaptive tone mapping), see `sepolicy/vendor/mediacodec.te`

Known issue: the QTI decoder can reject the output format the Dolby client
sets. It logs `QC2V4l2Codec: ? is not a supported pixel format!` for format 34
in surface mode, or `YV12 is not a supported pixel format!` in ByteBuffer
mode, then `Failed to set resolution and buffer size for OUT port` and
`Configuring hw component failed`. The stock A14 `libqcodec2_v4l2codec` has the
same check and message, so it is not specific to the 6.6 libraries. Its V4L2
pixel format names are NV12, NV12 UBWC, P010, TP10 UBWC and RGBA8888 UBWC, and
`Created 0 filters for pipelined codec c2.qti.hevc.decoder` is logged for every
session. `vendor.qc2.log.msg`, `vendor.qc2.log.trace` and
`vendor.qc2.log.buffers` raise the QC2 logging.

Debugging:

```
logcat -s dolby-c2-service dolbyvision-qti-c2-hidl DolbyC2Client C2DolbyHevcDec QC2V4l2Codec QC2Interface
dumpsys media.codec | grep -i dolby
```

Device wiring
-------------
Inherit `hardware/dolby/dolby.mk` and keep the manifest/matrix variables
`+=` in BoardConfig. `dolby.mk` installs VINTF manifests via
PRODUCT_COPY_FILES, so set `BUILD_BROKEN_VINTF_PRODUCT_COPY_FILES := true`.
Include the codecs from your media_codecs XMLs:

```xml
<Include href="media_codecs_dolby_audio.xml" />
```

Audio effects (`audio_effects_config.xml`, v2.0). The AIDL effect factory
needs the Sony type UUID to resolve the effects:

```xml
<library name="dap" path="libswdapaidl.so"/>
<library name="dvl" path="libdlbvolaidl.so"/>
<library name="gamedap" path="libswgamedapaidl.so"/>

<effect name="dap" library="dap" uuid="9d4921da-8225-4f29-aefa-39537a04bcaa" type="fa81de0e-588b-11ed-9b6a-0242ac120002"/>
<effect name="dlb_music_listener" library="dvl" uuid="40f66c8b-5aa5-4345-8919-53ec431aaa98" type="fa81de0e-588b-11ed-9b6a-0242ac120002"/>
<effect name="dlb_ring_listener" library="dvl" uuid="21d14087-558a-4f21-94a9-5002dce64bce" type="fa81de0e-588b-11ed-9b6a-0242ac120002"/>
<effect name="dlb_alarm_listener" library="dvl" uuid="6aff229c-30c6-4cc8-9957-dbfe5c1bd7f6" type="fa81de0e-588b-11ed-9b6a-0242ac120002"/>
<effect name="gamedap" library="gamedap" uuid="3783c334-d3a0-4d13-874f-0032e5fb80e2" type="fa81de0e-588b-11ed-9b6a-0242ac120002"/>
```

marble keeps the stock `volume_listener` helpers on the post-process streams
instead of `dlb_music_listener` (see `device_xiaomi_sm8450-common` a212830).
vendor_sony_dolby additionally defines `dlb_notification_listener`
(`1f0091e3-6ad8-40fe-9b09-5948f9a26e7e`); not used here.

The stock `libqtigefar` is kept, so the audio HAL must initialize its legacy
API (`hardware_qcom_audio-ar` 2cd9e3cabc73).

